option.CAMERA_PL.comment=Circular polarizer (C-PL) in front of the lens. Deepens the blue sky (strongest 90 degrees from the sun, no effect near the sun, opposite it or in rain) and removes reflections from water, glass, leaves and other glossy surfaces to show what is beneath (water clears most at about 53 degrees, less from straight above or at grazing angles; metal reflections stay). Wide angles show an uneven sky. It costs 1.5 stops with Camera exposure and 1 stop with Manual exposure; P, A, S and Auto exposure make it up. The look works with every exposure mode
option.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION=Polarizer Rotation
option.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.comment=Rotation of the polarizer ring (degrees). 0 removes horizontally polarized light the most (reflections on water and the ground, the horizon sky with the sun high), 90 removes vertically polarized light the most (such as the sky with a low sun to the side). Turning it 90 degrees swaps the effect
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.0=0°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.15=15°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.30=30°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.45=45°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.60=60°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.75=75°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.90=90°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.105=105°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.120=120°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.135=135°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.150=150°
value.CAMERA_PL_ROTATION.165=165°
option.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_LIGHT=Real-World Light Ratios
option.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_LIGHT.comment=Only for Camera exposure. Sets the brightness of the moon, night sky, block lights, glowing surfaces and the Nether and End ambient light to real-world ratios against the sun, so real camera settings work by day and by night (e.g. sunny day f/16, 1/100 s, ISO 100; lit town at night f/8, 2 s, ISO 400). When off, night scenes need settings close to daytime ones
screen.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_OPTIONS=Real-World Light Ratios
screen.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_OPTIONS.comment=For Camera exposure, brings lights other than the sun closer to real-world ratios
option.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_NIGHT=Night Brightness
option.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_NIGHT.comment=Brightness in stops for the moon, stars, night sky and the minimum light in caves and on moonless nights. Lower is darker and needs longer exposures or higher ISO. Stars, night sky and the minimum light drop further than the moon (-8 and -16 stops by default) and never go below the default. 0 restores all three to the game brightness, bright compared to the sun
option.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_BLOCKLIGHT=Light Source Brightness
option.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_BLOCKLIGHT.comment=Stops applied to light from blocks, held items, entities and lava, and to the Nether and End ambient light. Lower is darker around lights
option.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_EMISSION=Glowing Surface Brightness
option.EXPOSURE_PHYSICAL_EMISSION.comment=Stops for how bright light blocks, flames and glowing eyes look (the light they cast is unchanged). Higher glows visibly by day too, but at night clips widely with larger bloom and bokeh
option.EXPOSURE_SPEED_BRIGHT=Adaptation Speed (to Bright)
option.EXPOSURE_SPEED_DARK=Adaptation Speed (to Dark)
option.EXPOSURE_SPEED_BRIGHT.comment=How fast exposure follows a brighter scene (per second; 3 takes about 1/3 s for most of the change).
option.EXPOSURE_CAMERA_SPEED_BRIGHT=Camera AE Speed (to Bright)
option.EXPOSURE_CAMERA_SPEED_BRIGHT.comment=How fast Camera P, A, S and M with Auto ISO follow a brighter scene with the shutter speed, f-number and ISO (per second). Same default as the auto exposure adaptation
option.EXPOSURE_CAMERA_SPEED_DARK=Camera AE Speed (to Dark)
option.EXPOSURE_CAMERA_SPEED_DARK.comment=How fast Camera P, A, S and M with Auto ISO follow a darker scene (per second; 1 takes about 1 s for most of the change, about 5 s to settle when walking into a torch-lit cave)
option.EXPOSURE_SPEED_DARK.comment=How fast exposure follows a darker scene (per second; 1 takes about 1 s for most of the change).
option.EXPOSURE_MIN_EV=Minimum Exposure
suffix.EXPOSURE_MIN_EV= EV
option.EXPOSURE_MAX_EV=Maximum Exposure
suffix.EXPOSURE_MAX_EV= EV
option.EXPOSURE_NIGHT_LIFT_STRENGTH=Night Lift
option.EXPOSURE_NIGHT_LIFT_STRENGTH.comment=How strongly auto exposure brightens moonlit nights by metering the dark parts of the screen. 0 keeps the night dark. Sky, water and moon path change by the same amount. Where many lights are in view (lit villages and towns) only a small fixed lift remains, with any metering. No effect with manual exposure.
option.EXPOSURE_METERING_MODE=Metering
option.EXPOSURE_METERING_MODE.comment=Which part of the screen auto exposure measures. Evaluative: the whole screen weighted toward the center; outdoors at night it darkens the picture (up to 1 EV) when lit areas would blow out. Center-weighted: about three quarters of the weight in a circle one third of the screen width. Spot: only a circle one ninth of the screen width. Average: the whole screen evenly. Highlight-weighted: never brighter than what keeps the brightest few percent below white, so night scenes with lights get darker. Partial: only a circle 0.22 of the screen width (about 6% of the screen, like Canon's partial metering). In the automatic Camera modes (P, A, S) Evaluative leaves out the darkest shadows and the brightest few percent and never meters brighter than EV 16 1/3 (like Nikon's matrix metering), without the night darkening; the other modes measure the whole weighted area. No effect with manual exposure, Camera M or the VHS / DV response.
value.EXPOSURE_METERING_MODE.0=Evaluative
value.EXPOSURE_METERING_MODE.1=Center-weighted
value.EXPOSURE_METERING_MODE.2=Spot
value.EXPOSURE_METERING_MODE.3=Average
value.EXPOSURE_METERING_MODE.4=Highlight-weighted
value.EXPOSURE_METERING_MODE.5=Partial
option.EXPOSURE_INFO_MODE=Exposure Display
option.EXPOSURE_INFO_MODE.comment=For Camera P, A, S and M with Auto ISO. Shows the shutter speed, f-number and ISO in small text at the top center, rounded to 1/3 stops. When the camera cannot reach the right exposure (shutter at 30 s or 1/8000, lens wide open or at its smallest aperture) the value at its limit blinks, like in a real camera's viewfinder: shutter speed in A, f-number in S, both in P, ISO in M with Auto ISO
value.EXPOSURE_INFO_MODE.0=Off
value.EXPOSURE_INFO_MODE.1=Blink at Limit
value.EXPOSURE_INFO_MODE.2=Always
option.EXPOSURE_DR=Dynamic Range Correction
option.EXPOSURE_DR.comment=Like the automatic dynamic range feature of phones and recent cameras. Exposes darker when bright areas would blow out, then brings mid-tones back and lifts dark areas. Can leave a glow or dark ring around bright objects and make shadows look flat. Light cost (also runs the bloom downsample when Bloom is off). No effect with manual exposure or the VHS / DV response.
option.EXPOSURE_RESPONSE_MODE=Exposure Response
option.EXPOSURE_RESPONSE_MODE.comment=How auto exposure follows the scene. VHS (1980s-90s camcorder): center-weighted, a slow iris that overshoots and hunts, and gain that rises in the dark (up to 12 dB). DV (2000s camcorder): average metering, follows slowly, blows out for a moment when entering a bright place, gain up to 18 dB. Meant for the Analog filter (its noise follows the gain). Metering, night lift and indoor dimming are not used.
value.EXPOSURE_RESPONSE_MODE.0=Modern
value.EXPOSURE_RESPONSE_MODE.1=VHS
value.EXPOSURE_RESPONSE_MODE.2=DV
